// The refusal this feature could most easily have got wrong.
//
// A folded constant reaches the backend as a literal, so there is no `mul` instruction left
// for the run-time overflow trap to fire on. If the fold wrapped, this program would compile
// and put -2 in the binary, and nothing downstream could notice — the exact silent wrong
// answer the language exists to refuse, produced BY the compiler rather than caught by it.
//
// So both compilers ask whether the multiply is safe BEFORE doing it. They have to: Burxt's
// own `*` panics on overflow, so "compute it and look at the sign" would take the compiler
// down instead of reporting the mistake.
const MAX: Int = 9223372036854775807;
const TWICE: Int = MAX * 2;
print(TWICE);
